value.en:
On 23 November 2025, parliamentary and presidential elections were held.

On 26 November 2025, before the election results were announced, the military (calling themselves the High Military Command for the Restoration of National Security and Public Order, ACM) announced that they had seized control of the country and suspended the electoral process. 

On 27 November 2025, General Horta Nta Na Man, a close ally of ousted President Embaló, was sworn in as interim president for one year.

On 8 December 2025, the ACM published the transitional political charter, which provides for a Transitional National Council (CNT). 

The CNT, which took office on 12 December 2025, is composed of the following 65 members and the Speaker, Major-General Tomás Djassi:
-	10 people designated by the president of the transitional republic;
-	15 representatives of the high military command;
-	20 representatives of political parties;
-	11 representatives of civil society;
-	9 representatives of the regions.

The transitional political charter stipulates a 12-month transitional period (Article 19.1) and bars the interim president and interim prime minister from running in the next elections (Article 23).

On 13 January 2026, the CNT approved a new constitution. Under that constitution, the President of the Republic retains the power to dissolve Parliament, which will be renamed from the People's National Assembly to the National Assembly.

On 21 January 2026, the transitional president issued decree No. 02/2026, setting a general election for 6 December 2026.

value.fr:
Le 23 novembre 2025 se sont tenues des élections législatives et présidentielles.

Le 26 novembre 2025, avant l’annonce des résultats des élections, l’armée (sous le nom de Haut commandement militaire pour le rétablissement de la sécurité nationale et de l’ordre public, ACM) a annoncé qu’elle avait pris le contrôle du pays et suspendait le processus électoral.

Le 27 novembre 2025, le général Horta Nta Na Man, proche allié du Président renversé Embaló, a prêté serment en tant que Président par intérim pour un an.

Le 8 décembre 2025, l’ACM a publié la Charte politique de transition, qui prévoit la création d’un Conseil national de transition (CNT).

Le CNT, qui a pris ses fonctions le 12 décembre 2025, est composé des 65 membres suivants, qui siègent aux côtés du Président du parlement, le général de division Tomás Djassi :
- 10 personnes désignées par le Président de la République de transition ;
- 15 représentants du haut commandement militaire ;
- 20 représentants des partis politiques ;
- 11 représentants de la société civile ;
- 9 représentants des régions.

La charte politique de transition prévoit une période de transition de 12 mois (article 19.1) et interdit au Président et au Premier Ministre par intérim de se présenter aux élections suivantes (article 23).

Le 13 janvier 2026, le CNT a approuvé une nouvelle Constitution, en vertu de laquelle le Président de la République conserve le pouvoir de dissoudre le parlement, qui sera rebaptisé Assemblée nationale au lieu d’Assemblée nationale populaire.

Le 21 janvier 2026, le Président de transition a publié le décret n° 02/2026, qui fixe la date des élections générales au 6 décembre 2026.

value.en:
No party won the 41 seats needed to form a government after the snap elections in 2023. The new party “Europe now!”, led by former Finance Minister Milojko Spajić (see note 1), become the first force with 24 seats in the 81-member parliament. The “Together! For the future that belongs to you” coalition, which includes the leading Democratic Party of Socialists (DPS), became the second force with 21 seats. The “For the future of Montenegro” coalition ended in third place with 13 seats, while the remaining 23 seats were distributed among 6 other parties. 

Lengthy negotiations to form a new government followed. Although the newly elected Parliament held its first session on 27 July, it failed to elect a new Speaker. On 10 August, President Jakov Milatović nominated Mr. Spajić (Europe Now!) as prime minister designate tasked to form a government. On 31 October, Mr. Spajić (Europe Now!) formed a coalition government (see note 2). At 36, he is the youngest Prime Minister in Europe. 

The early elections in 2023 were the first to be held under the new President Milatović (Europe now!) who had succeeded Mr. Milo Đukanović (DPS) in May 2023. They followed a political deadlock and the collapse of two governments. On 16 March, the then President Đukanović (DPS) dissolved Parliament in view of early elections. The key electoral issues included Montenegro’s European Union accession, anticorruption measures and economic development. 

Note 1:

Mr. Spajić served under the government of Prime Minister Zdravko Krivokapić who had led the 11-party opposition coalition “For the Future of Montenegro” in the 2020 parliamentary elections. After he lost in a vote of no confidence in February 2022, the then Deputy Prime Minister Dritan Abazović became the Prime Minister in April 2022. However, Mr. Abazović also lost in a vote of no confidence four months later. He stayed as a caretaker Prime Minister until the 2023 snap elections.  

Note 2:

The new Government comprises the following parties. 

- Europe Now! movement (Pokret Evropa sad), which was part of the Europe Now! coalition;

- Democrats of Montenegro (Demokrate Crne Gore), which was part of Bravery Counts! (HRABRO se broji!); 

- Socialist People’s Party of Montenegro (Socijalistička narodna partija Crne Gore);

- CIVIS (which was part of the Europe Now! electoral list);

- Albanian Forum – Besa for European development (Albanski forum – Besa za evropski razvoj);

- Albanian Alliance (Albanska alijansa).

The “For the Future of Montenegro” coalition does not have members in the Government of Montenegro but is part of the parliamentary majority. The coalition voted in favour of the Government and its member is the President of Parliament.

